Alloy: server: save the registry from a child process, so programs don't stop while it is written (macOS).

Every 30 seconds wineserver rewrites each registry branch that changed, the whole file, and it answers no request
while it does. Every program in the prefix stops for that long: 26 to 27 ms with a 3.3 MB system.reg under
Rosetta 2, three times in a 95 second test, and a prefix with Steam in it has a registry several times that size.
In a game that is a dropped frame or a hitch twice a minute. (Found by the MacGamingFixes project, which holds the
saves back with a wrapper around wineserver.)

The periodic save now forks: the child writes the registry as it was at the fork and exits, and the server goes on.
The server marks the saved branches clean at the fork, so changes made meanwhile go into the next save, and marks
them dirty again if the child fails. Saving at exit waits for a child still writing, then saves as before, so the
files on disk are never older than what a synchronous save would have left. A child still running after a whole
period is killed and that save is done in the server.

Measured with the same test: the stops are 7 ms for the first save and 2.7 ms after it (the fork itself).

--- a/server/mach.c
+++ b/server/mach.c
@@ -53,7 +53,8 @@
 
 void sigchld_callback(void)
 {
-    assert(0);  /* should never be called on MacOS */
+    /* the only child the server has on macOS is the one saving the registry */
+    reap_registry_save();
 }
 
